  "comments": [
    {
      "author": "lubossaloky",
      "created_at": "2026-09-26",
      "text": "Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar party has been polling as the largest party in most recent surveys.\n\nSee also [Gadi Eisenkot widens the gap against Benjamin Netanyahu on Polymarket](https://www.i24news.tv/en/news/israel-election-s-2026/artc-gadi-eisenkot-widens-the-gap-against-benjamin-netanyahu-on-polymarket). The predictive platform gives the Yashar party a 62% chance of winning the most seats in the Knesset, compared to 39% for Likud.",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "Theomachado",
      "created_at": "2026-09-22",
      "text": "@(Koboh) Glad my analysis could help :), thank you for the feedback!",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "Koboh",
      "created_at": "2026-09-22",
      "text": "@(Theomachado) That’s a fair point, and I appreciate you explaining the distinction. I agree that simply averaging the polls can hide the underlying split between the two groups. The track record of the different pollsters is also important context, especially when their historical performance has differed. I’ll take a closer look at the weighting and methodology you mentioned. Thanks for adding that perspective.",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "Theomachado",
      "created_at": "2026-09-22",
      "text": "@(Koboh)\n\n@Koboh Good summary, but I'd read that 18–31 range on Likud differently. It isn't noise. The polls split into two camps: the six mainstream houses have Likud at 19–22, while Direct Polls (i24) and Channel 14 have it at 27–31. Averaging them hides that.\n\nThe two camps also have different track records. Direct Polls has leaned about 3 seats toward Likud relative to the field in every election since 2019, and overstated Likud by about 4 seats at this stage in 2021 and 2022. So the question is less \"how close is the race\" and more \"which camp is right,\" and that's where I'd put the analysis. I lay out the history, possible biases and my weighting in my comment above.",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "Russell-viper",
      "created_at": "2026-09-22",
      "text": "Predictions markets are slightly favouring Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar party but in Israel it's not simply about the winner takes all. A prime minister can come from any party that finishes second.&#x20;\n\n| Prediction markets | Yashar | Likud | &#xA;Other/Naftali's Together party |\n| ------------------ | ------ | ----- | ----------------------------------- |\n| **Polymarket**     | 53%    | 29%   | 8%                                  |\n| **Kalshi**         | 54%    | 26%   | 8%                                  |\n\nLet me expand on this\n\n* Israeli don't directly select a president, they vote for parties which then fill 120 Knesset seats. Seats are allocated proportionally to parties based on the electoral threshold.\n* &#x20;After the election, if one parties secures a majority which means securing 61 Knesset seats, happens to form a sole government, if not then that person negotiates with other parties to form a coalition.&#x20;\n* Sole government is rare in Israel as their electoral system is proportional and produces many other parties. **No Israeli party has ever won the 61 seats required for sole party government.** In 2022 Israeli election, Likud won 32 of 120 Knesset seats with 23.4% of the vote, Netanyahu then formed a government with allied parties and reached 64 overall seats.&#x20;\n\n\nPolymarkets predictions for # of seats\n\n&#x20;                   Eisenkot's Yashar\n\n<img height=\"500\" width=\"530\" src=\"https://cdn.metaculus.com/user_uploaded/image_NhF80Kw.png\" />\n\n&#x20;                 Netanyahu's Likud\n\n<img height=\"380\" width=\"536\" src=\"https://cdn.metaculus.com/user_uploaded/image_ysFs2I0.png\" />\n\n&#x20;The incumbent party Likud may have some advantages but there are all sort of other things that work against them. I think Yasher has slight advantage here.  &#x20;",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "Koboh",
      "created_at": "2026-09-22",
      "text": "This is a really tough one to call. The polls are very close, with Yashar and Likud taking the lead in different polls. I wouldn’t rely too much on any single poll because the numbers can change quickly. The bigger issue may be the coalition math, not just who finishes first. Smaller parties crossing or missing the 3.25% threshold could make a real difference in the final seat count. There’s also still plenty of time for things to shift, so for me, this remains a very open race rather than a settled one.\n\n| Party / bloc              | Current MEF average | Mean across 15 polls | Range |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------- | -------------------- | ----- |\n| Yashar                    | 24                  | 22.9                 | 20–25 |\n| Likud                     | 23                  | 22.5                 | 18–31 |\n| Together                  | 12                  | —                    | —     |\n| Democrats                 | 10                  | —                    | —     |\n| Joint List                | 8                   | —                    | —     |\n| Otzma Yehudit             | 8                   | —                    | —     |\n| Shas                      | 8                   | —                    | —     |\n| United Torah Judaism      | 8                   | —                    | —     |\n| Yisrael Beytenu           | 8                   | —                    | —     |\n| Religious Zionism + Zehut | 6                   | —                    | —     |\n| Ra’am                     | 5                   | —                    | —     |\n| Netanyahu bloc            | 53                  | —                    | —     |\n| Opposition parties        | 54                  | —                    | —     |\n| Arab parties              | 13                  | —                    | —     |\n\nThese are the tracker figures based on the newest poll from each polling house over the previous 14 days, with fieldwork through September 17.\n\nData source:\n\n[https://israelvote.meforum.org/](https://israelvote.meforum.org/)",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "johnnycaffeine",
      "created_at": "2026-09-21",
      "text": "@(Theomachado) Thanks for flagging. We have added a tiebreaker: the party of the next prime minister. Failing that, it is the sole tied party with at least one other minister in the government. The fallback after that (such as if no government forms) is the party that gets the most votes.&#x20;",
      "prediction": null
