      "text": "## My Forecast\n\nI'm at **Yashar 55% / Likud 44% / Other 1%**.\n\n***\n\n## **Resolution Criteria Importance**\n\nTo tackle this question, we need to make sure we understand that we are **dealing with different events than the Polymarket or the Kalshi pool**. The main difference is how the markets handle a possible tie: Polymarket breaks ties by valid votes, and Kalshi by which party enters government; in this question, we treat a tie as an ambiguous result.\n\n***\n\n## **My initial approach**\n\nMy background is in economics, so my first instinct was to model this as an economic-voting problem: voters as agents punishing or rewarding the incumbent based on their material situation. I tested it on the 12 Knesset elections from 1996 to 2022, regressing the seat change of the prime minister's party (and separately of Likud) on five indicators for the year before the vote:\n\n* GDP growth\n* GDP per capita growth\n* private consumption growth\n* unemployment (level and change)\n* CPI inflation\n\nNothing came close to significance, with every p-value above 0.45 and every R² below 0.06. In addition a outlier election war with the ongoing war would not yield confident results.\n\n***\n\n## **MAIN ANALYSIS**\n\nThis time, after abandoning the econ base thesis, I want to model the seven prior election pools to understand their timeline significance using 25-50 days out from the final week and the actual outcome. Although the 25-50 period shows more error and variance, the data is more consistent. An outside view taken alone suggests a 3-seat mainstream Yashar lead; however, bellow we are going to dive deeper into the actual 2026 polls direction and history.\n\n<img height=\"772\" width=\"1488\" alt=\"image.png\" src=\"https://codahosted.io/docs/1KXudU0jcK/blobs/bl-X8-a7BL0Sw/50a8ba6e67a860098ca896094d94aff3dc1be98dacd791692687b62b07da024735e28739b008dab544349af0aa0dac3b6f726a59953c36839662f932fb20d5f22324435fc5d1d8e5e663e52ce49de2b9708d8cb8393e70683a14fa8ff6702aee9b290b7b\" />\n\n### This is where most of my forecast comes from. The question is really about which pollsters are right. Across all 311 polls published this year, the pollsters split into two camps.\n\n* **Six mainstream pollsters** (Channel 12, Kan, Maariv, Channel 13, and others) have **Yashar ahead by about 3 seats**.\n* **Direct Polls (i24) and Filber (Channel 14)**, both with roots in Netanyahu's circle, have **Likud ahead by 5 to 10 seats**.\n\nWhile researching this question, I learned the difference between these polls: the mainstream ones are totally independent firms hired to be impartial. As Direct Polls and Filber: Direct Polls was co-founded by Shlomo Filber, a former Netanyahu aide and Likud campaign manager.\n\nAs we can see in this graph:\n\n<img height=\"767\" width=\"1481\" alt=\"image.png\" src=\"https://codahosted.io/docs/1KXudU0jcK/blobs/bl-W3SShigGLl/e412aa579d377c72fc6308b5088bcc1d88599d33017962cad2b62b1453596e6083df28218d3494c55d49f6a84b2904cba86022c7c745d1e0eef98c471a8bc0fd830727ca3bf1fcc892e8b8b490eac19a90dc8d94d22674b917308be0de1fab76e05015d2\" />\n\nThe chart compares how far off each pollster group was on Likud's margin roughly a month before each of the last five contests; bars above zero mean the polls understated Likud, and bars below zero mean they overstated it. Direct Polls gave Likud a better margin than the mainstream pollsters in all five elections (about 3 seats on average) and overstated Likud by about 4 seats in both 2021 and 2022, suggesting a consistent pro-Likud lean.\n\nTherefore, it is important to keep that historical lean in mind when analysing the following data:\n\n<img height=\"768\" width=\"1377\" alt=\"image.png\" src=\"https://codahosted.io/docs/1KXudU0jcK/blobs/bl--EAonaRreu/e68e17f11282b2fc71762cfea7c58a34ec5e9c9fa9edb55c3dc95f685db69467d47795b9ad9b8dc248fe021600ea351cea3f7fe3af87f574684f2ae6cb6c297b96bc0a595b998e32b8f8910a8d40998214c032303c987f76587ae25f26d624ad04427862\" />\n\n**Above are all the 2026 polls so far. As expected, Direct Polls and Channel 14 lean toward a better result for Likud, showing it ahead by 5 to 10 seats, while the mainstream pollsters say the opposite and have Yashar up by about 3. Both sides agree Likud's lead has collapsed since January. They just disagree on where it stands today.**\n\nI give the mainstream group about **two-thirds** of the weight. It's six independent firms, and they got the last two elections right. The other **third goes to the pro-Likud pollsters**, because for all their lean, they were the ones closer to the truth when the mainstream polls badly underestimated Likud in 2019 and 2020. I'm not comfortable ignoring them the way the market seems to. (Kalshi 61% for Yashar and Polymarket 63% for Yashar)\n\n[https://polymarket.com/event/israeli-legislative-election-winner](https://polymarket.com/event/israeli-legislative-election-winner)\n\n[https://kalshi.com/markets/kxisraelknesset](https://kalshi.com/markets/kxisraelknesset)\n\nThat weighting, plus the usual polling error, puts me at Yashar 55 / Likud 44.",
